title: '[pallet-revive] Measure basic block compilation in its benchmark'

doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    The `basic_block_compilation` benchmark now measures what `pallet-revive` charges it for:
    compiling one maximum-sized basic block. Every call into a PVM contract is charged
    `basic_block_compilation(1) - basic_block_compilation(0)` on top of the per-byte code load
    cost. Before, both values of the component ran the same contract, so the slope did not
    measure the compilation.

    The benchmark now calls a contract whose entry block has 2 instructions for `b = 0` and
    `BASIC_BLOCK_SIZE` instructions for `b = 1`. Both contracts contain the same instructions in
    a different order, so they have the same size and cause the same storage reads. The slope
    is the time the interpreter needs to compile the additional instructions of the larger
    block.

    The `WeightInfo` trait is unchanged. Runtimes that use their own `pallet_revive` weights
    should regenerate them: until they do, the per-call compilation charge stays at the value
    their last run fitted. The charge changes once the weights are regenerated.

    `VmBinaryModule::with_entry_block_size` and `VmBinaryModule::MIN_ENTRY_BLOCK_SIZE` are added
    to the `call_builder` module, which is only available with the `runtime-benchmarks` feature.
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    The weight charged for each call into a PVM contract changes once a runtime regenerates its
    `pallet_revive` weights, because the per-call basic block compilation charge is now
    measured by its benchmark.

crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: minor
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: patch
